                  {
                    "a": "(a) Quantitative Analysis and Qualitative Analysis: In qualitative analysis, the elements, ions or compounds present in a sample are identified, while in quantitative analysis the amount of a substance present in a sample is determined. Qualitative analysis does not measure the quantity, while quantitative analysis measures the quantity. For example, a flame test is qualitative analysis and titrimetric analysis is quantitative analysis. (b) Titrimetric Analysis and Gravimetric Analysis: In titrimetric analysis, the volume of a solution of known concentration (titrant) is measured to find the amount of an unknown substance, while in gravimetric analysis the analyte is converted into some product and its weight is measured to find the amount. Titrimetric analysis is volumetric analysis and gravimetric analysis is weight analysis; gravimetric analysis is the most accurate analysis.",
                    "q": "1. Distinguish between following: (a) Quantitative Analysis and Qualitative Analysis (b) Titrametric Analysis and Gravimatric Analysis"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "[[T]]Feature|Classical Method|Instrumental Method;;Procedure|Procedure is simple and accurate|Procedure is sensitive and technical;;Equipment|Equipment needed is cheap|Equipment needed is expensive;;Basis of measurement|Methods are based on absolute measurement|Methods are based on reliable measurement;;Training|Specialized training is not required|Specialized training is required;;Accuracy|Accuracy decreases by decreasing amount|Accuracy depends upon instruments;;Speed|Determination is slow|Determination is very fast;;Amount of sample|Large amount of sample is needed|Small amount of samples can be used[[/T]]",
                    "q": "2. Prove that instrumental analytical methods are more effective then classical analytical methods?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Gas chromatography is a technique used in analytical chemistry for the separation of volatile compounds. The word gas chromatography is clear from its word that it is used for the separation of gases and volatile liquids in gaseous state. The separation takes place by the exchange between a mobile gas phase and a liquid or solid stationary phase. The first gas chromatograph was introduced by Nobel Prize winner John Porter Martin in 1950 and he is considered the father of modern gas chromatography. The instrument of gas chromatography consists of a gas cylinder, sample injector, gas chromatograph, detector and data collection device. Gas is the mobile phase and the gas cylinder controls the gas passage up to the sample injector, which proceeds toward a two-columned gas chromatograph; it is a stationary phase with uniform temperature. When a compound reaches the detector, it detects the elution and sends signals to the data collection device (computer). Volatile compounds are those organic compounds which have high vapor pressure and low water solubility and emit gases from certain solids and liquids, such as benzene, formaldehyde, xylene and toluene. Gas chromatography is used in the analysis of inorganic compounds, carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, vitamins, pollutants like benzene, plastic materials and dairy products.",
                    "q": "3. Describe Gas Chromatography in detail?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Electrochemical methods depend upon electrochemical cells because in these methods the electrical properties of a substance, such as electrical charge, electrical potential and electrical current, are measured, and these measurements are made with an electrochemical cell. An electrochemical cell consists of two half cells, both connected with an electrode (anode and cathode), and each electrode is dipped in electrolytic solution, which is ZnSO4 at the anode and CuSO4 at the cathode. The half cells are connected by means of a salt bridge (NaCl) which provides a platform for ionic connectivity without mixing. The anode possesses a negative sign due to the liberation of electrons in the oxidation reaction and the cathode possesses a positive sign due to consumption of electrons in the reduction reaction. For example, in the cell Zn(s) | ZnSO4(aq) || CuSO4(aq) | Cu(s), zinc is the anode and copper is the cathode; a voltmeter measures the electrical force. Electrochemical cells are of two types: the electrochemical cell (which produces electricity from a chemical reaction) and the electrolytic cell (which uses electricity to cause a chemical reaction). Potentiometry and conductometry work with the help of these cells.",
                    "q": "4. Justify that electrochemical methods depend upon Electrochemical Cells?"
                  }
