                    "a": "(a) Gamma rays come from radioactive materials: they are produced when the nuclei of unstable atoms decay into a stable nucleus or lose energy. (b) The Gamma Knife Radiosurgery is a medical procedure that uses gamma rays to destroy small tumors in the brain with less damage to surrounding cells: high-energy gamma rays are directed at the cancerous tumor to kill cancer cells in oncology. (c) Positron Emission Tomography (PET) is a functional medical imaging method: a short-lived positron-emitting radioactive sample is injected into the body; the radiated positrons fuse with nearby electrons and produce two gamma rays of 511 keV travelling in opposite directions; a computer generates an image that highlights the location of the biological process being examined (for example, brain tumors). Gamma rays are also used to treat cancer (radiotherapy).",
