                    "a": "Importance of glues and epoxy resins in our society: The use of different synthetic adhesives like glue and epoxy resins is common. Synthetic adhesives are substances that stick to the surface of other objects; the material like plastic, wood, metal, ceramic, glass and rubber on which glue is applied is called the substrate. Epoxy adhesive is more expensive as compared to resin glue; both are synthetic adhesives and require mixing before use, but epoxy hardens much faster than resin glue. We can use adhesive any time to reattach broken objects. Poly vinyl acetate is a common white glue, used in book binding. Polyurethane glue is a flexible adhesive, used in fixing the soles to the bodies of shoes and in wood working. Natural rubber bonds to the substrate on contact and is used in self-adhesive envelopes. Conductive adhesive is commonly used in electronics to repair equipment. Epoxy glue contains epoxy; it forms strong bonds with glass, plastics, plywood, laminated boards and ceramic. Commonly, epoxy resins are used where water resistance is required; bridges, dams and power stations are also coated with epoxy resins. The excellent adhesive properties of epoxy resins are due to the attractive forces between the epoxy resin and the surface of the substrate; epoxy resin is used in the construction of vehicles, trucks, boats and aircrafts, and its drying time is 6–30 minutes.",
