                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "Enzymes are biocatalysts which facilitate chemical reactions by lowering the activation energy. They are special molecules made up of mostly protein, and the name was coined when yeast was introduced into fruit sap, which converted it into alcohol (En = inside, zyme = yeast). Characteristics of enzymes: (1) Enzymes are biocatalysts, made mostly of proteins, three-dimensionally folded chains of amino acids with a specific shape, determined by the sequence of amino acids. They speed up reactions by bringing reactants together and reducing the activation energy. (2) When an enzyme starts a chemical reaction, it catalyzes the reaction but is not used up itself, so even a single or small amount of enzyme can catalyze a reaction fast. (3) Their presence does not affect the nature or properties of the end products. (4) The reactants of enzymes are called substrates. (5) They are very specific in their action; a single enzyme catalyzes only a single chemical reaction or a group of related reactions. (6) A small portion of the enzyme where the substrate attaches is called the active site, whose shape is complementary to the substrate. (7) They are sensitive to even a minor change in pH, temperature and substrate concentration. (8) Some enzymes require a cofactor for functioning. (9) Many enzymes work in a sequential manner to produce a specific product, and this pathway is called a metabolic pathway. (10) The activity of enzymes can be enhanced by activators and decreased by inhibitors.",
                    "q": "What are enzymes? Describes characteristics of enzymes."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The factors affecting the activity of enzymes are substrate concentration, temperature and pH. (1) Substrate concentration: If the amount of enzyme is kept constant and the substrate concentration is gradually increased, the reaction velocity increases until it reaches a maximum (the point of saturation), after which further increase produces no significant change, because the enzyme molecules are saturated with substrate. (2) Temperature: Enzyme activity occurs within a narrow range of temperatures. As temperature increases, the rate of reaction first increases because of increased kinetic energy and more collisions between enzyme and substrate. However, increasing temperature also increases vibrations and the structure of the enzyme is lost, that is, the enzyme is denatured, so the rate of reaction decreases or may stop completely. The optimum temperature for humans is close to 37°C. (3) pH: All enzymes work at their maximum rate in a narrow range of pH. The point where the enzyme is most active is known as the optimum pH. For example, pepsin works at a low pH (highly acidic), while trypsin works at a high pH (basic). Most enzymes work at a neutral pH of 7.4. Small changes in pH above or below the optimum do not cause a permanent change, but extreme changes in pH can cause enzymes to denature and permanently lose their function.",
                    "q": "Describe factors affecting enzyme activity."
                  }
                ],
