                    "a": "The principle of moments states that when a body is in equilibrium, the sum of the clockwise moments about any point is equal to the sum of the anticlockwise moments about that point. The book prints '3 m'; a distance of 3 m is impossible on a metre rule, so this looks like a printing error and the intended reading is 3 cm. [[S]](i) Equate the moments: 12 × d = 20 × 3[[/S]][[E]]d = 60/12 = 5 cm[[/E]][[S]](ii) If the 20 N force is at 4 cm: force × 5 = 20 × 4[[/S]][[E]]force = 80/5 = 16 N[[/E]][[A]](i) The 12 N force will be at a distance of 5 cm on the other side of the pivot (with the printed 3 m the answer would be 5 m, which is impossible on a metre rule), (ii) a force of 16 N should be placed to replace the 12 N force.[[/A]]"
