          {
            "label": "Exercise 10.4",
            "items": [
              {
                "q": "1. In the adjacent figure, O is the mid point of line segments AB and CD.\nProve that AC ≅ BD\n[Hint: Join point A to point C and point B to point D. Prove △AOC ≅ △BOD by S.A.S. ≅ S.A.S.]",
                "a": "O is the mid-point of AB\nAO ≅ BO\nO is the mid-point of CD\nCO ≅ DO\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD\nIn △AOC and △BOD:\nAO ≅ BO\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD\nCO ≅ DO\n△AOC ≅ △BOD\nAC ≅ BD"
              },
              {
                "q": "2. Two line segments bisect one another. Prove that the line segments joining their end-points are congruent. [This is the general statement of problem 1 above]",
                "a": "AB and CD bisect one another at O\nAO ≅ BO\nCO ≅ DO\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD\nIn △AOC and △BOD:\nAO ≅ BO\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD\nCO ≅ DO\n△AOC ≅ △BOD\nAC ≅ BD\n∠AOD ≅ ∠BOC\nIn △AOD and △BOC:\nAO ≅ BO\n∠AOD ≅ ∠BOC\nDO ≅ CO\n△AOD ≅ △BOC\nAD ≅ BC"
              },
              {
                "q": "3. Prove that the diagonals of a rectangle are congruent.\n[Hint: Let AC and BD be the diagonals of rectangle ABCD. Prove that △ABC ≅ △BAD by S.A.S. Theorem]",
                "a": "ABCD is a rectangle\nAB ≅ AB\n∠ABC = ∠BAD = 90°\nBC ≅ AD\nIn △ABC and △BAD:\nAB ≅ BA\n∠ABC ≅ ∠BAD\nBC ≅ AD\n△ABC ≅ △BAD\nAC ≅ BD"
              },
              {
                "q": "4. In an Isosceles triangle, the bisector of the angle at the vertex angle is the right bisector of the base. Prove.",
                "a": "In △ABC, AB ≅ AC\nAD is the bisector of ∠A meeting BC at D\n∠1 ≅ ∠2\nIn △ABD and △ACD:\nAB ≅ AC\n∠1 ≅ ∠2\nAD ≅ AD\n△ABD ≅ △ACD\nBD ≅ DC\n∠ADB ≅ ∠ADC\nm∠ADB + m∠ADC = 180°\nm∠ADB = 90° = m∠ADC\nAD ⊥ BC\nAD is the perpendicular bisector of BC"
              },
              {
                "q": "5. Every point on the perpendicular bisector of a line segment is equidistant from the end-points of the line segment.\n[Here OP is the perpendicular bisector of AB. We have to prove that AP ≅ BP. So join AP and BP, then prove that triangles AOP and BOP are congruent by S.A.S. Theorem]",
                "a": "OP is the perpendicular bisector of AB\nO is the mid-point of AB\nAO ≅ BO\nOP ⊥ AB\nm∠AOP = 90° = m∠BOP\nIn △AOP and △BOP:\nAO ≅ BO\n∠AOP ≅ ∠BOP\nOP ≅ OP\n△AOP ≅ △BOP\nAP ≅ BP"
              },
              {
                "q": "6. Medians to the congruent sides of an isosceles triangle are congruent.\nGiven: In △ABC, AB ≅ AC and BD, CE are medians\nTo Prove That: BD ≅ CE",
                "a": "D is the mid-point of AC\nAD = [[1/2]]AC\nE is the mid-point of AB\nAE = [[1/2]]AB\nAB ≅ AC\nAD ≅ AE\nIn △ABD and △ACE:\nAB ≅ AC\n∠A ≅ ∠A\nAD ≅ AE\n△ABD ≅ △ACE\nBD ≅ CE"
              },
              {
                "q": "7. From a point not on the line, one and only one perpendicular can be drawn to the line.",
                "a": "C is any point outside AB\nCD ⊥ AB\nm∠1 = 90°\nm∠1 + m∠2 = 180°\nm∠2 = 90°\nCB is also perpendicular on AB\nIn △DBC, m∠B < m∠2\nm∠B < 90°\nCB is not perpendicular on AB\nCD is the only perpendicular on AB"
              },
              {
                "q": "8. Consider the figure given here and discuss the following questions/statements with your class-mates and try to find out the answers of the questions asked here.\n(i) What relationship do you suggest between the pair of angles 1, 2 and 3, 4?\n(ii) Are ∠1 and ∠4 congruent? Are ∠3 and ∠2 congruent? If not, why? Under what conditions they may be congruent?\n(iii) If ∠3 and ∠2 are congruent, what will happen to the two converging lines?\n(iv) ∠1, ∠2 and ∠3, ∠4 are supplementary angles. Do you agree? Give reasons. Are ∠1, ∠3 and ∠2, ∠4 also supplementary angles. If not, suggest a condition when these may be supplementary.\n(v) Find the condition when the pair of interior angles on the same side of a transversal are supplementary.\n(vi) If the converging lines are produced, they will meet at some point. What new figure will be developed? Out of four interior angles of converging lines which two of them will become the exterior angles of the triangle? Which exterior angle is greater than m∠2, and which exterior angle is greater than m∠4?",
                "a": "(i) m∠1 + m∠2 = 180°\nm∠3 + m∠4 = 180°\n(ii) ∠1 ≇ ∠4\n∠3 ≇ ∠2\nThe two lines are not parallel\n∠1 ≅ ∠4 and ∠3 ≅ ∠2 when the two lines are parallel\n(iii) The two lines are parallel\n(iv) ∠1 and ∠2 are supplementary\n∠3 and ∠4 are supplementary\n∠1 and ∠3 are not supplementary\n∠2 and ∠4 are not supplementary\n∠1 + ∠3 = 180° and ∠2 + ∠4 = 180° when the two lines are parallel\n(v) The interior angles on the same side of a transversal are supplementary when the two lines are parallel\n(vi) A triangle is formed\n∠1 and ∠3 are the exterior angles of the triangle\nm∠3 > m∠2\nm∠1 > m∠4"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Exercise 10.5",
            "items": [
              {
                "q": "1. In each of the figures given below, find the angle marked x. Numbers indicate the degree measure of the angle.\n(a) Two parallel lines cut by a bent line; 130° and 40° marked.\n(b) Two parallel lines cut by a bent line and a transversal; 75° marked and 115° printed at the bend. [The book's Hint and Answers use 135° at the bend.]\n(c) A six-sided figure; 25°, 115°, 105°, 30° marked and two angles marked x, one at the notch on the left and one at the right point.\n(d) Two lines crossing, cut by two parallel lines; 40° and 75° marked.\n[Hint: From (a) to (d) through the vertex of angle marked x (or 135°) draw a line parallel to both the lines]",
                "a": "(a) 180° − 130° = 50°\n50° + 40° = 90°\nx = 90°\n(b) 135° − 75° = 60°\n180° − 60° = 120°\nx = 120°\n(c) Notch: 25° + 30° = 55°\nx = 55°\nRight point: 180° − 115° = 65°\n180° − 105° = 75°\n65° + 75° = 140°\nx = 140°\n(d) 180° − 40° − 75° = 65°\nx = 65°"
              },
              {
                "q": "2. If two line segments AB and CD bisect one another at point O, prove that AC is parallel to BD and AD is parallel to BC. [This problem has previously been solved. After proving the two triangles congruent use Theorem 7 to prove the lines to be parallel using alternate angles congruent.]",
                "a": "AO ≅ BO\nCO ≅ DO\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD\nIn △AOC and △BOD:\nAO ≅ BO\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD\nCO ≅ DO\n△AOC ≅ △BOD\n∠OAC ≅ ∠OBD\nAC ∥ BD\n∠AOD ≅ ∠BOC\nIn △AOD and △BOC:\nAO ≅ BO\n∠AOD ≅ ∠BOC\nDO ≅ CO\n△AOD ≅ △BOC\n∠OAD ≅ ∠OBC\nAD ∥ BC"
              },
              {
                "q": "3. Points D and E are the mid-points of the sides AB and AC of a △ABC. If DE is produced to point F so that EF ≅ DE, prove that CF ∥ AB and DE ∥ BC.\nGiven: Points D and E are the mid-points of the sides AB and AC of △ABC. DE is produced to point F so that EF ≅ DE\nTo Prove That: CF ∥ AB and DE ∥ BC.",
                "a": "In △ADE and △CFE:\nAE ≅ CE\n∠AED ≅ ∠CEF\nDE ≅ EF\n△ADE ≅ △CFE\n∠A ≅ ∠ECF\nCF ≅ AD\nAD ≅ BD\nCF ≅ BD\n∠A and ∠ECF are alternate angles\nCF ∥ AB\nCF ∥ BD\nCF ≅ BD\nBCFD is a parallelogram\nDF ∥ BC\nDE ∥ BC\nCF ∥ AB and DE ∥ BC"
              },
              {
                "q": "4. Prove that opposite angles of a parallelogram are congruent.\n[Hint: Use Cor. 2 of Theorem 8, i.e., m∠A + m∠B = 180° etc.]",
                "a": "ABCD is a parallelogram\nAB ∥ CD\nAD ∥ BC\nm∠A + m∠B = 180°\nm∠B + m∠C = 180°\nm∠A = m∠C\nm∠B + m∠C = 180°\nm∠C + m∠D = 180°\nm∠B = m∠D\n∠A ≅ ∠C\n∠B ≅ ∠D"
              },
              {
                "q": "5. How many degrees are there in an angle of an equilateral triangle?",
                "a": "△ABC is equilateral\nAB ≅ BC ≅ AC\n∠A ≅ ∠B ≅ ∠C\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C = 180°\n3 × m∠A = 180°\nm∠A = 60°\nm∠B = 60°\nm∠C = 60°"
              },
              {
                "q": "6. Find measure of an angle which is equal to the sum of measures of other two angles of a triangle.\n[Hint: By Theorem 9, Cor. 3, the sum of other two angles is 90° which is the same as the angle of a right angled triangle, so measure of the angle is 90.]",
                "a": "m∠A = m∠B + m∠C\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C = 180°\nm∠A + m∠A = 180°\n2 × m∠A = 180°\nm∠A = 90°"
              },
              {
                "q": "7. The interior angles of a quadrilateral are together equal in measure to four right angles.\n[Hint: Draw any one diagonal. Use Theorem 9 to prove that 2 rt ∠ + 2 rt ∠ = 4 rt ∠s].",
                "a": "ABCD is a quadrilateral\nDiagonal AC\nIn △ABC:\nm∠BAC + m∠BCA + m∠B = 180°\nIn △ADC:\nm∠DAC + m∠DCA + m∠D = 180°\nm∠BAC + m∠DAC = m∠A\nm∠BCA + m∠DCA = m∠C\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C + m∠D = 180° + 180°\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C + m∠D = 360°\n= 4 rt ∠s"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Review Exercise",
            "items": [
              {
                "q": "1. Demonstrative Geometry is a branch of Mathematics. What does it do?",
                "a": "Statements concerning geometrical figures (theorems) are proved through logical reasoning."
              },
              {
                "q": "2. Write what you know about the following terms:\n(a) Undefined Terms (b) Assumptions or Fundamental Agreements\n(c) Propositions or Theorems (d) Postulates\n(e) Axioms (e) Corollaries",
                "a": "(a) Undefined Terms: Terms accepted without definition\nPoint, Line, Plane, Space\n(b) Assumptions or Fundamental Agreements: Statements accepted without proof\n(c) Propositions or Theorems: Statements proposed for discussion\nA proposition proved true is a theorem\n(d) Postulates: Fundamental Agreements related to geometrical figures\n(e) Axioms: Fundamental Agreements related to numbers\n(f) Corollaries: Facts which readily follow from a proved theorem"
              },
              {
                "q": "3. Write down the four Basics of Reasoning.",
                "a": "1. Undefined Terms\n2. Defined Terms\n3. Assumptions or Fundamental Agreements\n4. Propositions"
              },
              {
                "q": "4. (a) State Theorem 1 and prove it.\n(b) The sum of measures of all the angles at a point in a given straight line on the same side of it is equal to two right angles. Prove.",
                "a": "(a) If a straight line stands on another straight line, the sum of measures of two angles so formed is equal to two right angles.\nOC stands on AB\nm∠AOC + m∠BOC = 2 rt ∠s\nThrough O, draw OP ⊥ AB\nm∠AOP = 1 rt ∠ = m∠BOP\nm∠AOC = m∠AOP + m∠POC\nm∠BOC = m∠BOP − m∠POC\nm∠AOC + m∠BOC = m∠AOP + m∠BOP\n= 1 rt ∠ + 1 rt ∠\n= 2 rt ∠s\n(b) OC and OD stand on AB on the same side of it\nm∠AOD + m∠DOC + m∠BOC = 180°\nm∠AOC + m∠BOC = 180°\nm∠AOC = m∠AOD + m∠DOC\nm∠AOD + m∠DOC + m∠BOC = 180°"
              },
              {
                "q": "5. (a) Prove that if two lines intersect, then the opposite vertical angles are congruent.\n(b) An equilateral triangle is also equiangular, prove.",
                "a": "(a) AB and CD intersect at O\n∠BOC ≅ ∠AOD\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD\nm∠AOC + m∠BOC = 180°\nm∠AOD + m∠AOC = 180°\nm∠AOC + m∠BOC = m∠AOD + m∠AOC\nm∠BOC = m∠AOD\n∠BOC ≅ ∠AOD\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD\n(b) In △ABC, AB ≅ BC ≅ AC\nBC ≅ AC\n∠A ≅ ∠B\nAB ≅ AC\n∠B ≅ ∠C\n∠A ≅ ∠B ≅ ∠C"
              },
              {
                "q": "6. (a) From a point not on the line, one and only one perpendicular can be drawn to a given line.\n(b) If a transversal intersects two parallel lines, the alternate angles so formed are congruent.",
                "a": "(a) C is any point outside AB\nCD ⊥ AB\nm∠1 = 90°\nm∠1 + m∠2 = 180°\nm∠2 = 90°\nCB is also perpendicular on AB\nIn △DBC, m∠B < m∠2\nm∠B < 90°\nCB is not perpendicular on AB\nCD is the only perpendicular on AB\n(b) l ∥ m, transversal EF intersects them at P and Q\n∠1 ≅ ∠PQR\nQR does not lie on m\nl ∥ QR\nl ∥ m\nl is parallel to two lines m and QR\n∠1 ≅ ∠2\n∠3 ≅ ∠4"
              },
              {
                "q": "7. (a) The measure of an exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of the measures of non-adjacent interior angles of the triangle.\n(b) State Theorem 5 and prove it.",
                "a": "(a) △ABC\nAC produced to D\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠ACB = 180°\nm∠ACD + m∠ACB = 180°\nm∠ACD = m∠A + m∠B\n(b) If two sides of a triangle are congruent, then the angles opposite to these sides are congruent.\nIn △ABC, AB ≅ AC\n∠B ≅ ∠C\nAD bisector of ∠A, D on BC\n∠1 ≅ ∠2\nIn △ABD and △ACD:\nAB ≅ AC\n∠1 ≅ ∠2\nAD ≅ AD\n△ABD ≅ △ACD\n∠B ≅ ∠C"
              },
              {
                "q": "8. (a) Write 'True/False' against each statement:\n(i) If two lines intersect, then vertical adjacent angles are congruent.\n(ii) Through a point not on a line, one and only one line can be drawn parallel to the line.\n(iii) Sum of the measures of three angles of a triangle may be equal to 180°.\n(iv) If a ray is drawn through a point of a given line and forms two adjacent angles, then these angles are complementary to one another.\n(v) In a right angled triangle the acute angles are complementary.\n(B) Fill in the blanks with appropriate words:\n(i) Three non-collinear points determine a ___.\n(ii) Two distinct points determine a ___.\n(iii) One and only one ___ can be drawn from a point not on the line.\n(iv) If a line cuts two parallel lines, then each pair of ___ angles and each pair of ___ angles are congruent.\n(v) Measure of an exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the ___ of the measures of the non-adjacent interior angles of the triangle.",
                "a": "(a) (i) False\n(ii) True\n(iii) False\n(iv) False\n(v) True\n(B) (i) Plane\n(ii) Line\n(iii) Perpendicular\n(iv) Alternate, Corresponding\n(v) Sum"
              },
              {
                "q": "9. (i) In the figure on the right, m∠a = m∠b = m∠c = m∠d. Write down four pairs of parallel lines.\n(ii) In each of the figures given below, find the angle marked x. Numbers indicate the degree measure of the angle.\n(a) Two parallel lines cut by a bent line; 20° and 42° marked.\n(b) Two parallel lines cut by a bent line and a diagonal; 130° and 75° marked.\n(c) A diagonal and a ray meeting a horizontal line; 120° and 40° marked.",
                "a": "(i) AB ∥ CD\nAB ∥ FG\nFG ∥ CD\nEF ∥ GC\n(ii) (a) 20° + x = 42°\nx = 42° − 20°\nx = 22°\n(b) 180° − 130° = 50°\n75° + 50° = 125°\nx = 125°\n(c) 180° − 120° = 60°\n60° − 40° = 20°\nx = 20°"
              }
            ]
          }
