              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "Three ways to manage a conflict: 1. Communication — the constructive response to conflict; both parties listen to each other's position and interests. 2. Negotiation — two or more people decide an agreement step by step; effective or 'win-win' negotiation fulfils the interests of all parties. 3. Compromising — both parties leave something and gain something; a solution which fulfils some interests of both parties. Other ways: Avoiding, Accommodating, Collaborating, Mediation and Arbitration.",
                    "q": "Suggest any three ways to manage a conflict."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Communication is the constructive response to conflict whereas violence is the extremely destructive response. Non-violent or constructive ways often lead to positive change — for example, conflict leads to better communication between individuals or to better social justice for backward groups. In the school context, conflict in the curriculum can attract the attention of students, increase the motivation to learn and develop critical thinking skills. In the egg example: both girls want the egg; the mother listened to their position and divided the egg in half, but if she had listened to their interests, the first girl would get the white part and the second girl the yolk — both would win. In this way communication prevents conflict.",
                    "q": "Give examples on how \"Communication\" can prevent a conflict?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Steps for effective negotiation: 1. Identify position and interests — each person defines what their position and interests are. 2. Present and listen — each person says what happens to cause the problem without blaming the other person; the other person listens and does not interrupt. Then the other person also tells in the same way. While speaking, each person states their position and interest as specifically as possible. 3. Brainstorm possible solutions — both parties consider all possible solutions without judging them. 4. Strike off unacceptable solutions — all ideas are read over again and those which are unacceptable are cancelled out. 5. Choose an acceptable solution — the remaining ideas are read out and both parties choose one solution which fulfils the interests of both as much as possible. 6. Action plan — after choosing the solution, decide how it will be put in place, paying attention to details such as who will do it and how.",
                    "q": "Describe the steps to be taken for an effective negotiation."
                  }
                ],
                "label": "2. Answer the following questions"
              }
