          {
            "name": "Additional Questions",
            "exercises": [
              {
                "label": "Cell Division",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What is cell division?",
                    "a": "The multiplication of new cells: cells are continuously dying and are replaced by new cells. The cell that divides is called the parent cell, and the new cells produced are called daughter cells."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the two parts of cell division?",
                    "a": "Nuclear division (karyokinesis) followed by the division of cytoplasm (cytokinesis)."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is the role of the nucleus in cell division?",
                    "a": "The nucleus takes part in cell division because it contains the hereditary material called chromosomes, which divide and form new cells."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the four stages of mitosis?",
                    "a": "Prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telophase."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What happens in prophase?",
                    "a": "Chromatin becomes thickened, shortened and visible under a microscope as chromosomes."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What happens in metaphase?",
                    "a": "Chromosomes get attached to spindle fibres formed by the two centrioles."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What happens in anaphase?",
                    "a": "Contraction of spindle fibres takes place, resulting in the chromosomes moving towards the opposite poles of the cell."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What happens in telophase?",
                    "a": "Chromosomes reach towards their respective poles, followed by cytokinesis, resulting in two daughter cells."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "How many daughter cells are produced in mitosis and in meiosis?",
                    "a": "Mitosis produces two identical daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes as the parent cell. Meiosis produces four daughter cells with half (haploid) the number of chromosomes."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Chromosomes, Genes and DNA",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What are chromosomes made of?",
                    "a": "Proteins and nucleic acid. A chromosome consists of two chromatids attached at the centre to the same centromere."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "How many chromosomes are there in a human cell?",
                    "a": "46. For any organism, the number of chromosomes present in the cell remains constant."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is a chromatid?",
                    "a": "One of the two parts of a chromosome, attached at the centre to the same centromere."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is a centromere?",
                    "a": "The part at the centre of a chromosome to which the two chromatids are attached."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is a gene?",
                    "a": "The basic physical and functional unit of heredity, located on chromosomes in a linear order, responsible for the expression of various characteristics."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is a nucleotide?",
                    "a": "The unit of DNA; a nucleotide consists of phosphate, sugar and base."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Name the four nitrogenous bases of DNA.",
                    "a": "Adenine, Guanine, Thymine and Cytosine."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is the shape of DNA?",
                    "a": "Two strands linked with each other like a ladder twisted around each other — a long spiral ladder (double helix)."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Heredity and Transmission of Characteristics",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What is heredity?",
                    "a": "The transmission of characteristics from parents to offspring."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is a gamete?",
                    "a": "A sex cell (male cell or egg cell) with a haploid (n) number of chromosomes, formed by meiosis."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is a zygote?",
                    "a": "The first cell of an organism, formed when a male cell (n = haploid) and an egg cell (n = haploid) fuse; it is a diploid (2n) cell in which the complete heredity material is restored."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Who was Gregory Mendel?",
                    "a": "A pioneer among geneticists who put forward the concept of inheritance of characteristics (traits) from parents to offsprings."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Give examples of inheritable characteristics.",
                    "a": "Eye colour, texture and colour of hair, skin colour, attached or free earlobes, tongue rolling, height, complexion, rolling of tongue, lobed or lobeless ear, and colour of eyes (black, blue, green, brown)."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Why do children resemble their parents?",
                    "a": "Because they inherit many characteristics from them — the offspring receives some genes from the female parent and some from the male parent, resulting in a different genetic make-up."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is the difference between somatic cells and sex cells in division?",
                    "a": "Somatic (body) cells divide by mitosis for growth, repair and development. Sex cells divide by meiosis for reproduction, producing gametes with half the number of chromosomes."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "How is the complete heredity material restored in the zygote?",
                    "a": "During meiosis the number of chromosomes is reduced to half in the gamete cells (n = haploid). When the male and egg gametes fuse, the diploid (2n) number is restored in the zygote."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "num": "2.3"
          }
