          {
            "name": "Additional Questions",
            "exercises": [
              {
                "label": "Air Pollution",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What is a pollutant?",
                    "a": "A harmful substance which damages the environment — its introduction into the environment causes pollution."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the sources of air pollution?",
                    "a": "Natural sources and manufactured (human) sources — for example, industrial and motor vehicle emissions, and the burning of coal, trees and fossil fuels."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is sulphur dioxide? What are its harmful effects?",
                    "a": "Sulphur dioxide is a poisonous gas, one of the main by-products of industrial and motor vehicle emission, with a terribly irritating smell. Constant exposure can lead to cough, sneeze, cold, shortness of breath, bronchitis and fatigue; it is the main reason for asthmatic attacks. Eye irritation, eye watering, shortness of breath and lung damage are other common problems."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is carbon monoxide? What are its harmful effects?",
                    "a": "Carbon monoxide is a colourless and odourless gas produced by the incomplete combustion of fuel. It mostly results in diseases related to the heart, like heart attacks and cardiovascular diseases, as well as headache and reduced mental awareness; in excess it may affect the lungs and weaken lung functions."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the harmful effects of oxides of nitrogen?",
                    "a": "Nitrogen dioxide is a main contributor of smog. Too much exposure can lead to various respiratory and lung diseases, infections, lung irritation and respiratory symptoms such as cough, chest pain and difficulty in breathing."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are chlorofluorocarbons (CFC)? What are their harmful effects?",
                    "a": "Chlorofluorocarbons are a group of gases widely used as refrigerants and industrial foaming agents. CFC is a major cause of the depletion of the ozone layer. Inhalation of high levels can affect the lungs, central nervous system, heart, liver and kidneys; symptoms include drowsiness, unclear speech, disorientation, tingling sensations and weakness in the limbs. Extremely high levels can result in death; ingestion can lead to nausea, irritation of the digestive tract and diarrhea."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "List the problems in the human organ system caused by air pollutants.",
                    "a": "Lung diseases and bronchitis; breathing problems and shortness of breath; brain damage and reduced mental awareness; headaches; eye irritation and watering; heart attacks and cardiovascular diseases; fatigue and low productivity."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is acid rain? How is it formed?",
                    "a": "Power stations and other industrial units run by burning coal emit carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxide into the air, which can dissolve in rain water to form acid rain."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Greenhouse Effect, Global Warming and Ozone Layer",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What is global warming?",
                    "a": "The increase in the average temperature of the surface of the earth, caused by the greenhouse effect — greenhouse gases trap the sun's heat in the atmosphere."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Which gases contribute to the greenhouse effect?",
                    "a": "Carbon dioxide (the major greenhouse gas), methane, nitrous oxide and CFC (chlorofluorocarbon)."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the consequences of acid rain on living and non-living things?",
                    "a": "Acid rain affects the photosynthesis and growth of plants; when it falls in rivers and lakes it can kill aquatic organisms; it destroys manmade structures, metals and statues of archaeological importance, kills many animals, destroys agricultural land and causes damage to crops and plants; it also releases toxic metals into the forests that stunt the growth of trees."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is the ozone layer? Where is it present?",
                    "a": "A layer of ozone gas (O_3) present high up in the atmosphere, between twelve to fifty kilometres above the ground."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What causes the depletion of the ozone layer?",
                    "a": "The release of chlorine atoms into the atmosphere breaks down the ozone shield. The major source of chlorine is chlorofluorocarbon (CFC)."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the effects of ozone depletion?",
                    "a": "Ultraviolet light penetrates and reaches the earth's surface and causes skin cancer and many other lethal effects on many organisms including humans."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "How can the greenhouse effect be demonstrated by a model?",
                    "a": "Fill two glasses of equal size each with 2 cups of cold water and put five ice cubes in each; wrap one glass in a plastic bag and seal it tightly. Place both glasses in the sun for one hour, then measure the water temperature in each glass. The glass covered in plastic is warmer because the bag trapped heat in the glass, in the same way the greenhouse gases trap heat in the atmosphere."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is the role of trees in reducing the greenhouse effect?",
                    "a": "Trees help to mitigate carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gas emissions; when they are cut, burned or removed, they become carbon sources instead."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Deforestation and Conservation of Earth",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "Define deforestation.",
                    "a": "The permanent destruction of forests to make the land available for other uses."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the major causes of deforestation?",
                    "a": "Overpopulation, paper, mining, logging, agriculture expansion and climate change."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the effects of deforestation on the environment?",
                    "a": "(1) Increased greenhouse gas emissions. (2) Acidic oceans — the oceans become more acidic with the increased supply of carbon dioxide, putting ocean species and ecosystems at extreme risk. (3) Loss of species — giant pandas, rhinos and the Asian elephant are just a few of hundreds of endangered species due to deforestation. (4) Climate control — removing trees leads to extreme temperature swings harmful to plants and animals. (5) Flooding and erosion — without trees, erosion sweeps land into nearby rivers and contaminates the water supply, greatly decreasing the quality of drinking water. (6) Life quality decrease — millions of people depend on forests for hunting, small-scale agriculture and medicine, and deforestation disrupts their lives and leads to migration."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Why are forests vital to our earth?",
                    "a": "Trees purify our air, filter our water, prevent erosion, act as a buffer against climate change, offer a home to plant and animal species, and provide natural resources such as medicine, food, timber and fuel."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is conservation of natural resources?",
                    "a": "Conservation of the quality of the environment and its resources — trees, water and energy — which are essential for the survival of present and future generations."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What can an individual do to save the earth?",
                    "a": "Make people aware and set an example for others; use reusable and recycled material (paper bags in place of plastic bags); avoid the unnecessary use of energy (switch off lights and fans, use stairs instead of lifts); reduce water wastage and reuse water wherever possible."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What can organizations and the government do to save the earth?",
                    "a": "Plantation, forestation and protecting wild life by banning products made from the skin and body of animals; use technology to develop environment-friendly materials, products and processes; use filters or scrubbers on industrial chimneys to remove sulfur dioxide; less use of fertilizers and pesticides."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "num": "4.4"
          }
