          {
            "name": "Additional Questions",
            "exercises": [
              {
                "label": "Digestive System",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What is digestion?",
                    "a": "The process of breaking down large and complex food particles into smaller, simpler and diffusible form."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Name the two types of digestion.",
                    "a": "Physical (mechanical) digestion and chemical digestion."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are the five steps of digestion?",
                    "a": "Ingestion, digestion, absorption, utilisation and egestion."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "How long is the alimentary canal in a living person?",
                    "a": "About seven metres."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Name the organs of the alimentary canal in order.",
                    "a": "Mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine and anus."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Name the accessory digestive organs.",
                    "a": "Teeth, liver, gall bladder and pancreas."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "How long does food take to complete its journey inside the body?",
                    "a": "24 to 30 hours."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is a bolus?",
                    "a": "The soft, flexible semi-solid food formed in the mouth when chewed food is mixed with saliva."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is chyme?",
                    "a": "The semi-liquid food formed in the stomach when the bolus is mixed with gastric juice."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is the function of bile?",
                    "a": "Bile, secreted by the liver and stored in the gall bladder, breaks down large-sized fats into smaller pieces so that fats can be digested faster by enzymes."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Where are water and mineral salts absorbed?",
                    "a": "In the large intestine."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Respiratory System",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What is respiration?",
                    "a": "A biochemical process during which simple carbohydrates like glucose are broken down to release energy."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Write the word equation for respiration.",
                    "a": "Glucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is breathing?",
                    "a": "A physical process in which oxygen is taken inside the body and carbon dioxide is given out from the body."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What are inhalation and exhalation?",
                    "a": "The taking in of oxygen is called inhalation and the giving out of carbon dioxide is called exhalation."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is gaseous exchange?",
                    "a": "The exchange of gases between alveoli and blood capillaries."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Name the parts that help in breathing.",
                    "a": "Ribcage, diaphragm and thoracic cavity (chest cavity)."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What happens to the diaphragm and the ribcage during inhalation?",
                    "a": "The diaphragm flattens and the ribcage moves out, so the chest cavity enlarges and air is drawn in."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What is anaerobic respiration?",
                    "a": "Respiration without oxygen, as in small microorganisms like fungi and bacteria. It is used in the production of vinegar, cheese and bread."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "Name three common respiratory disorders discussed in the chapter.",
                    "a": "Cold, tuberculosis and asthma."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "What causes tuberculosis and how is it transmitted?",
                    "a": "Tuberculosis is caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It is transmitted from person to person via nasal secretion and sputum."
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "How can respiratory disorders be prevented?",
                    "a": "By eating fresh fruits and vegetables, doing regular exercise, keeping clean, living in a pollution-free environment, getting proper vaccination and not smoking."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "num": "1.5"
          }
