          {
            "exercises": [
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "[[T]]Feature|Arteries|Veins;;Function|Carry blood away from the heart|Carry blood towards the heart;;Walls|Thick, elastic and muscular|Thin, with little elastic tissue and muscle;;Lumen|Small|Large;;Blood pressure|High|Low;;Blood carried|Oxygenated blood, except the pulmonary artery|De-oxygenated blood, except the pulmonary vein;;Valves|No valves|Valves present to prevent backflow[[/T]]",
                    "q": "(i) Arteries and veins"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "[[T]]Feature|Atria|Ventricles;;Position|The two smaller upper chambers of the heart|The two lower chambers of the heart;;Walls|Thin walls|Thick muscular walls;;Function|Receive blood through veins|Pump blood out of the heart;;Blood received|Right atrium receives de-oxygenated blood from the body; left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs|Right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium; left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium[[/T]]",
                    "q": "(ii) Atria and ventricles"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "[[T]]Feature|Oxygenated blood|Deoxygenated blood;;Oxygen|Rich in oxygen|Rich in carbon dioxide;;Colour in figures|Shown red|Shown blue;;Carried by|Pulmonary vein and aorta (to all body parts)|Vena cava and pulmonary artery (to the lungs);;Where it changes|Formed in the lungs and in capillaries after exchange|Formed in the capillaries near the cells[[/T]]",
                    "q": "(iii) Oxygenated and deoxygenated blood"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "5"
              }
            ],
            "name": "Differentiate between the following",
            "num": "2.5"
          },
          {
            "exercises": [
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "The supply of food, water and oxygen, and the collection of waste materials such as carbon dioxide from our body.",
                    "q": "What is transportation?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The system in which blood is pumped out of the heart through blood vessels to all parts of the body and back to the heart.",
                    "q": "What is the blood circulatory system?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Arteries, veins and capillaries.",
                    "q": "Name the three kinds of blood vessels."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Their thin, single-layered walls allow the exchange of oxygen, water and nutrients from blood to cells, and waste products from cells into the blood.",
                    "q": "What is the function of capillaries?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Four to five litres.",
                    "q": "How many litres of blood does the human body contain?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The heart is a hollow, cone-shaped and muscular organ located between the lungs and behind the sternum. It is divided into four chambers — two atria above and two ventricles below — separated into right and left sections by a septum.",
                    "q": "Describe the structure of the heart."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "About 70 to 75 times per minute.",
                    "q": "How many times does the heart beat per minute at rest?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "A chronic medical condition in which the blood pressure in the arteries is persistently increased. Normal resting blood pressure in an adult is approximately 120/80 mm Hg.",
                    "q": "What is hypertension?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Chest pain that occurs if an area of heart muscle does not get enough oxygen-rich blood. It is a symptom of coronary heart disease.",
                    "q": "What is angina?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "A common disorder in which the blood glucose (sugar) level is high and the body does not process food properly for use as energy. Its symptoms include excessive thirst, frequent urination, weight loss and extreme hunger.",
                    "q": "What is diabetes?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The replacement of a dysfunctional organ with a healthy one.",
                    "q": "What is transplantation?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Transport in Humans"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "Xylem transports water and minerals from the roots to the leaves via the stem, while phloem transports sugar produced in the leaves to all parts of the plant.",
                    "q": "What are xylem and phloem?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "By osmosis — water molecules move from a region of higher concentration (soil) to a region of low concentration (root hair) through the semi-permeable cell membrane.",
                    "q": "How does water enter the root hair?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Tubular outgrowths of the outermost layer of the root. Being long, narrow and single-celled, they increase the surface area for efficient absorption of water and mineral salts from the soil.",
                    "q": "What are root hairs?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "A force involved in the movement of water and dissolved minerals up in the xylem.",
                    "q": "What is root pressure?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The evaporation of water from the aerial parts of the plant, especially through the leaves (stomata).",
                    "q": "What is transpiration?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The pulling force generated when transpiration lowers the water inside the leaf cells, so water moves from the xylem to the leaf cells and is pulled up from the root all the way through the xylem.",
                    "q": "What is transpiration pull?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The movement of food materials from the leaves to other tissues throughout the plant.",
                    "q": "What is translocation?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Phloem — a long, continuous channel extending through the roots and stem into the leaves as veins.",
                    "q": "Through which tissue is food translocated in plants?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Xylem carries water and minerals only upwards, from the roots to the leaves. Phloem carries food from the leaves to the roots, stems, flowers and fruits, i.e. in both directions.",
                    "q": "Why is the flow in xylem unidirectional while in phloem it is bidirectional?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Sugar dissolved in water that moves in the phloem.",
                    "q": "What is sap?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Small openings in the leaves, especially through which transpiration takes place.",
                    "q": "What are stomata?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Transport in Plants"
              }
            ],
            "name": "Additional Questions",
            "num": "2.6"
          }
